    "created_at": "2026-10-27T16:40:20Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T16:40:20Z",
    "body": "> 5\\. I wonder what is going wrong with the existing `per-document.html` test then by the way: https://wpt.fyi/results/custom-elements/registries\n>         That uses some generic framework that attempts to test this, but I guess it does a poor job at it?\n\nPer spec [#shared-document-creation-infrastructure](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/document-lifecycle.html#shared-document-creation-infrastructure) step 6 we reuse the inner window when navigating away from initial `about:blank`. But browsers differentiate between transient/temporary `about:blank` (`window.open(foo).document`) and a more permanent / initial navigation to `about:blank` for which `load` is fired (`w = window.open().document; w.location=foo`). I think \nhttps://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/3267 is about that. Note that the spec has no concept of an initial navigation to `about:blank`, https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/12778.\n\nWe also have `custom-elements/revamped-scoped-registry/initial-about-blank.tentative.window.js` that is supposed to test this but has the same issue.\n\nI'll try to update `object-association.js`.\n\n---\n\n> Gecko and Blink have the old behavior, Webkit the new.\n\nWebKit also doesn't seem to associate the registry with the document and rather clears it on window reuse. I believe there are more differences between browsers in regard to what things they reset on the window when it's being reused. https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/3267#issuecomment-2894496042",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
